{"ex-402-1":{"number":"1","title":"Current through an electric car","question":"<p>An electric car is charged by a current of 80 A. Find the amount of charge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> flowing through the car in 2 minutes.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/b35360bf2eb2046b711a8787.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:269.9px\"/></p>","answer":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 80 × (2 × 60) = 9600 C</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 3","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":2},"id":"ex-402-1","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-2":{"number":"2","title":"Finding currents","question":"<p>Light bulbs are connected to a battery in different ways as shown.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/c1a096c77f8b514708535881.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:392.4px\"/></p><p>(a) In Figure a, the current through point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 1.5 A. Find the current through bulb <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, bulb <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and the battery.</p><p>(b) In Figure b, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 2.5 A and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 1 A, what are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Since they are all in series, the current through them is 1.5 A.</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> – <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 2.5 – 1 = 1.5 A</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 2.5 A</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 8","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":8},"id":"ex-402-2","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-3":{"number":"3","title":"Energy in a rechargeable battery","question":"<p>A 1.2-V rechargeable battery has a capacity of 2950 mA h. This means that it can provide a current of 2950 mA for 1 hour.</p><p>(a) How many coulombs of charges can be driven by the fully-charged battery to flow in a circuit?</p><p>(b) How much energy is stored in the fully-charged battery?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/ad694457b55cc66e8dfe7302.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:196.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (2950 × 10<sup>–3</sup> A)(3600 s) = 1.06 × 10<sup>4</sup> C</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (1.2)(1.06 × 10<sup>4</sup>) = 1.27 × 10<sup>4</sup> J</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":18},"id":"ex-402-3","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-4":{"number":"4","title":"Voltages in circuits","question":"<p>Find the voltages <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in the following circuits.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/6848b1c5652db4058fc42434.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:518.5px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 3 – 2 = 1 V</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 3 V</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 3 – 2 = 1 V</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Identify the parallel branches.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 16","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":27},"id":"ex-402-4","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-5":{"number":"5","title":"Resistance of a bulb","question":"<p>A light bulb is connected to a 3-V battery as shown. The current through the bulb is 0.3 A.</p><p>(a) Find the resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the bulb.</p><p>(b) How will the current change if the bulb</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2dc3782b9fb1a603d9e29007.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:173.6px\"/></p><p>is replaced by another one of a higher resistance?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 10 Ω</p><p>(b) Decrease</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, R ↑ ⇒ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ↓</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":38},"id":"ex-402-5","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-6":{"number":"6","title":"V−I graphs of ohmic conductors","question":"<p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graphs of two ohmic conductors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are shown on the right.</p><p>(a) Find the resistance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> have a lower or higher resistance than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/11a7afb47f110567e02bba60.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:257.4px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) For conductor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 25 V, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 10 A.</p><p>Resistance of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2.5 Ω</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a lower resistance than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 22","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":51},"id":"ex-402-6","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-7":{"number":"7","title":"I−V graph of a conductor","question":"<p>The p.d. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> across a conductor is plotted against the current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> passing through it at a constant temperature.</p><p>(a) Does the conductor obey Ohm’s law? How does the graph indicate this result?</p><p>(b) Find the resistance of the conductor when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is equal to (i) 2 V, (ii) 3 V.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/26c0c31030039640ce3eb870.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:203.9px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) No. The graph is not a straight line passing through the origin.</p><p>(b) (i) When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 V, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.5 A.</p><p>Resistance = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4 Ω</p><p>(ii) When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 3 V, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1 A.</p><p>Resistance = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 3 Ω</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 23","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":61},"id":"ex-402-7","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-8":{"number":"8","title":"Resistivity and resistance","question":"<p>A copper wire is 10 m long. Its cross-sectional area is 1.3 × 10<sup>–7</sup> m<sup>2</sup>. Its resistance is 1.34 Ω.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/dfcb934643c53976991d1442.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:395.7px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the resistivity of the copper wire.</p><p>(b) The length of the wire is reduced by half. How do its resistance and resistivity change?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) ρ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.74 × 10<sup>–8</sup> Ω m</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = ρ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> ∝ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> (for constant ρ and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>)</p><p>∴ When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is halved, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is also halved.</p><p>The resistivity remains unchanged.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Resistivity depends on the material but not the dimension.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 27","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":73},"id":"ex-402-8","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-9":{"number":"9","title":"Series connection","question":"<p>Two resistors are connected in series to a battery as shown.</p><p>(a) What is the equivalent resistance of the two resistors?</p><p>(b) What is the p.d. across each resistor?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/96bf069e1f18b6bed37dc3a4.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:161.0px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 2 + 4 = 6 Ω</p><p>(b) Current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> through resistors = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2 A</p><p>P.d. across 2-Ω resistor = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 × 2 = 4 V</p><p>P.d. across 4-Ω resistor = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 × 4 = 8 V</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 32","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":87},"id":"ex-402-9","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-10":{"number":"10","title":"Parallel connection","question":"<p>Two resistors are connected in parallel to a battery. The current drawn from the battery is 0.5 A.</p><p>(a) What is the equivalent resistance of the two resistors?</p><p>(b) Find the resistance of resistor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1fbfe02110b970d015fc6548.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:187.1px\"/></p><p>(c) Find the current passing through each resistor.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 12 Ω</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 20 Ω</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> through 30-Ω resistor = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.2 A</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> through resistor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.3 A</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 35","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":98},"id":"ex-402-10","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-11":{"number":"11","title":"Resistor network","question":"<p>Three resistors are connected to a battery.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/90e2be06f496e56ab2afe668.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:165.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p><p>(b) Find the p.d. across and the current through each resistor.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Consider the 4-Ω and 8-Ω resistor.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/babca71cdbe8da41a09f5a13.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:177.1px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>′ = 4 + 8 = 12 Ω</p><p>Consider the whole network.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/ea78401123c158a049a9e061.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:178.5px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 6 Ω</p><p>(b) P.d. across 12-Ω resistor = 6 V</p><p>Apply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> .</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> through 12-Ω resistor = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.5 A</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> through 4-Ω resistor = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> through 8-Ω resistor = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.5 A</p><p>Apply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>P.d. across 4-Ω resistor = 0.5 × 4 = 2 V</p><p>P.d. across 8-Ω resistor = 0.5 × 8 = 4 V</p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p>Problem-solving</p><p>Resistor network Finding equivalent resistance:</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">① Choose a partial network which is in series or parallel connection and calculate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for it.</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">② Replace that partial network with a single resistor of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"numbered-step\">③ Repeat the steps for the other parts.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The branch with the 4-Ω and 8-Ω resistors, and the branch with the 12-Ω resistor are parallel to the battery, so the p.d. across each branch is 6 V.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 37","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":112},"id":"ex-402-11","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-12":{"number":"12","title":"Brightness of two light bulbs","question":"<p>Light bulbs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> have resistances of 1 Ω and 2 Ω respectively.</p><p>(a) Which bulb is brighter in the following cases?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/9ed2c8a1de88509287676cfe.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:473.2px\"/></p><p>(b) Find the power delivered by the battery in the two cases.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) (i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 9 W</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4.5 W</p><p>∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is brighter.</p><p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1 A</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 1<sup>2</sup> × 1 = 1 W</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 1<sup>2</sup> × 2 = 2 W</p><p>∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is brighter.</p><p>(b) (i) Current through battery = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4.5 A</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 3 × 4.5 = 13.5 W</p><p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 3 × 1 = 3 W</p>","tips":"","think":"<p>How would the power of A change if one more bulb is added in parallel in (a)(i) and in series in (a)(ii)?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>Parallel: unchanged at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> because the ideal 3 V battery maintains the same voltage across A.</p><p>Series: it decreases. If the added bulb has resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>&gt;</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, the current becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>&lt;</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>&lt;</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 41","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":138},"id":"ex-402-12","chapter":"402"},"ex-402-13":{"number":"13","title":"The power consumed by a heater","question":"<p>Janet designs a heater by connecting a 10-Ω resistor to a 12-V battery.</p><p>(a) Find the power consumed by the heater.</p><p>(b) Ken suggests that by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, using a resistor</p><p>of higher resistance can increase the power</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/6527f48dc41d982ef254f37a.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:142.8px\"/></p><p>consumed by the heater. Comment on his suggestion.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 14.4 W</p><p>(b) His suggestion is incorrect.</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is constant, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ↑ ⇒ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ↓</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>↑</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>⇒</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>↓</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>It is not appropriate to use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in this case since both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> change.</p><p>Editorial check</p><p>The printed solution uses P = V²/R² in its explanatory line. The power relation is P = V²/R; the stated conclusion that power decreases as resistance increases remains correct at constant voltage.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 402  |  Source PDF p. 43","source":{"name":"402 Examples.docx","sha256":"638221277880396cf5fe768e9a79652cec59bb8dc5b696744338f84504be6399","block":160},"id":"ex-402-13","chapter":"402"}}